on Trade-Off L42:     3. cite32†4.3 Impact of Model Family and Scale L43:     4. cite33†4.4 Decoding Strategies: Beam Search vs. Sampling vs. WaterMax L44:     5. cite34†4.5 Entropy-Aware Detection L45:     6. cite35†4.6 Post-hoc Code Watermarking L46:       1. cite36†Experimental details. L47:       2. cite37†Metrics for code watermarking. L48:       3. cite38†Comparing watermarking methods. L49:       4. cite39†Comparing models. L50:     7. cite40†4.7 Multi-Lingual Robustness on Wikipedia L51:     8. cite41†4.8 Impact of Chunking Strategy on Long Documents L52:   6. cite42†5 Conclusion L53:   7. cite43†References L54:   8. cite44†A Additional Details on LLM Watermarking L55:     1. cite45†A.1 Hash Function L56:     2. cite46†A.2 Details on Watermark Schemes L57:       1. cite47†Green-list/red-list. L58:       2. cite48†SWEET. L59:       3. cite49†MorphMark. L60:       4. cite50†DiPMark. L61:       5. cite51†Gumbel-max. L62:       6. cite52†SynthID-text. L63:     3. cite53†A.3 Choice of the Secret Key and Statistical Correctness L64:       1. cite54†How to choose the secret key? L65:     4. cite55†A.4 Details on Radioactivity L66:       1. cite56†The Radioactivity Test Protocol. L67:       2. cite57†Teacher Forcing Setup. L68:       3. cite58†Test Statistic. L69:       4. cite59†WaterMax Is Not Radioactive. L70:   9. cite60†B Additional Text Samples L71:     1. cite61†B.1 Example of Code Tasks and Watermarked Codes L72:     2. cite62†B.2 Descriptive Text and Dialogue Preservation L73:   10. cite63†C Additional Results L74:     1. cite64†C.1 Analysis of Output Length Distributions L75: cite65†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org L76: \nL77: arXiv:2512.16904v1 [cs.CR] 18 Dec 2025\nL78: # How Good is Post-Hoc Watermarking With Language Model Rephrasing?\nL79: Pierre Fernandez Affiliation: FAIR, Meta Superintelligence Labs Core & Equal contributors.    Tom Sander Affiliation: FAIR, Meta Superintelligence Labs Core & Equal contributors.\nL80:    Hady Elsahar Affiliation: FAIR, Meta Superintelligence Labs    Hongyan Chang Affiliation: FAIR, Meta Superintelligence Labs    Tomáš Souček Affiliation: FAIR, Meta Superintelligence Labs    Valeriu Lacatusu Affiliation: FAIR, Meta Superintelligence Labs    Tuan Tran Affiliation: FAIR, Meta Superintelligence Labs    Sylvestre-Alvise Rebuffi Affiliation: FAIR, Meta Superintelligence Labs    Alexandre Mourachko Affiliation: FAIR, Meta Superintelligence Labs\nL81: ###### Abstract\nL82: Generation-time text watermarking embeds statistical signals into text for traceability of AI-generated content. We explore post-hoc watermarking where an LLM rewrites existing text while applying generation-time watermarking, to protect copyrighted documents, or detect their use in training or RAG via watermark radioactivity. Unlike generation-time approaches, which is constrained by how LLMs are served, this setting offers additional degrees of freedom for both generation and detection.\nL83: We investigate how allocating compute (through larger rephrasing models, beam search, multi-candidate generation, or entropy filtering at detection) affects the quality-detectability trade-off. Our strategies achieve strong detectability and semantic fidelity on open-ended text such as books. Among our findings, the simple Gumbel-max scheme surprisingly outperforms more recent alternatives under nucleus sampling, and most methods benefit significantly from beam search.\nL84: However, most approaches struggle when watermarking verifiable text such as code, where we counterintuitively find that smaller models outperform larger ones. This study reveals both the potential and limitations of post-hoc watermarking, laying groundwork for practical applications and future research.\nL85: ^{†}^{†}correspondence: tomsander@meta.com, pfz@meta.com^{†}^{†}Code: cite66†https://github.com/facebookresearch/textseal†github.com L86: ## 1 Introduction\nL87: Post-hoc text watermarking inserts an algorithmically-detectable signal into an existing text while remaining imperceptible to readers. It serves several goals such as copyright protection or traitor tracing. Early approaches relied on hand-crafted modifications: synonym substitutions (cite67†Topkara et al., 2006c ; cite68†Shirali-Shahreza and Shirali-Shahreza, 2008 ), grammatical transformations (cite69†Topkara et al., 2006b ; cite70†Topkara et al., 2006a ) or morphosyntactic alterations (cite71†Meral et al., 2009 ).\nL88: Recent methods based on deep neural networks follow a post-hoc paradigm where the input is the original text and the output is the watermarked text, using one watermark embedder and one watermark extractor (cite72†Abdelnabi and Fritz, 2021 ), similarly to state-of-the-art post-hoc watermarking approaches for images, audio, and video.\nL89: However, these methods are not effective, suffering from low capacity or unreliable detection, because they are highly constrained or easily broken by reversing the edits or synonyms.\nL90: With the emergence of large language models (LLMs), their popularization through ChatGPT (cite73†OpenAI, 2022 ), and growing concerns about potential risks (cite74†Crothers et al., 2022 ; cite75†Weidinger et al., 2022 ), generation-time text watermarking algorithms have been introduced to help detect AI-generated text (cite76†Aaronson and Kirchner, 2023 ; cite77†Kirchenbauer et al., 2023a ). These algorithms have been deployed at scale, for instance in Google’s Gemini with SynthID (cite78†Dathathri et al., 2024 ).\nL91: Most LLM watermarks alter the next token selection, for example, by promoting a specific set of tokens depending on previous tokens and a secret key. Detection of the watermark in a text is then performed through a theoretically grounded statistical test that provides rigorous guarantees over the false positive rates. In addition, these methods achieve high watermark power while adding minimal latency, by leveraging the entropy of the LLM to embed the signal.\nL92: However, in contrast with previous approaches, these methods require control at generation time and therefore cannot be applied to existing text.\nL93: A natural idea to extend these methods to post-hoc watermarking is to employ a paraphrasing LLM to re-generate the text, allowing the watermark to be injected at inference time without further model training. This approach has been explored in data protection literature (cite79†Jovanović et al., 2025 ; cite80†Sander et al., 2025 ; cite81†Rastogi et al., 2025 ; cite82†Zhang et al., 2025 ; cite83†Lau et al., 2024 ).\nL94: For example, “watermark radioactivity” exploits the fact that watermark signals leave detectable traces when watermarked text is used by another model, enabling active training and context membership inference (cite80†Sander et al., 2025 ; cite79†Jovanović et al., 2025 ). However, to our knowledge, there is no thorough evaluation of how post-hoc watermarking through LLM paraphrasing performs across different data domains, such as prose versus verifiable text like code.\nL95: Moreover, unlike generation-time watermarking where watermark embedding should not delay the generation, post-hoc watermarking allows trading additional compute for a better quality-detectability trade-off. This opens up several axes to explore: model size, watermarking method, decoding strategy (and even generating multiple candidates), as well as detection strategy.\nL96: cite84†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: Post-hoc text watermarking through watermarked LLM rephrasing. We do empirical evaluations and analyze detection power, semantic fidelity, and correctness according to different design choices such as watermark scheme and available compute (through the paraphrasing model and the decoding strategy).\nL97: To address this gap, in this paper, we provide a comprehensive evaluation of post-hoc watermarking. We find that current state-of-the-art LLM watermarking schemes applied in a post-hoc setting perform well on open-ended text, such as Wikipedia articles and books, achieving high detection power while preserving fidelity. However, these methods are less effective on verifiable text like code, where the requirement to preserve correctness severely constrains paraphrasing freedom.\nL98: Regarding design choices, we find that larger models better preserve semantics, while smaller models are necessary to effectively hide strong watermarks. Furthermore, the simplest Gumbel-max approach (cite76†Aaronson and Kirchner, 2023 ) dominates the Pareto frontier when classical random sampling is used. For other watermarking methods, we find that beam search decoding significantly improves the quality-detectability trade-off.\nL99: In short, our main contributions are:\nL100: \nL101:   * •\nL102: \nL103: A comprehensive evaluation: We conduct the first large-scale study of post-hoc watermarking through LLM rephrasing (cite85†Figure 1 ) across diverse domains, demonstrating that while current methods are effective for open-ended text, they struggle with verifiable formats like code.\nL104: \nL105:   * •\nL106: An analysis of design strategies: We isolate the impact of model size, decoding, and watermarking schemes. For instance, we identify Gumbel-max as the robust Pareto-optimal choice (cite86†Figure 2 ).\nL107: \nL108:   * •\nL109: \nL110: An open-source research framework: We release an easily modifiable codebase to facilitate research for post-hoc watermarking techniques.\nL111: ## 2 Related work\nL112: ### 2.1 Post-Hoc Text Watermarking\nL113: Early text watermarking altered text characteristics like characters or spacing (cite87†Brassil et al., 1995 ). Other methods modify grammatical or syntactical structures via pre-established rules (cite88†Topkara et al., 2005 ), including synonym substitution (cite67†Topkara et al., 2006c ) and word reordering through passivization or topicalization (cite69†Topkara et al., 2006b ; cite70†Topkara et al., 2006a ; cite71†Meral et al., 2009 ).\nL114: Text steganography follows similar principles (cite89†Winstein, 1998 ; cite90†Chapman et al., 2001 ; cite91†Bolshakov, 2004 ; cite68†Shirali-Shahreza and Shirali-Shahreza, 2008 ; cite92†Chang and Clark, 2014 ; cite93†Xiang et al., 2017 ). These edit-based systems typically exhibit low robustness and payload, e.g., 1-2 bits per sentence (cite94†Wilson and Ker, 2016 ).\nL115: Deep learning methods have since been used for this task, for instance, with masked language models for steganography (cite95†Ueoka et al., 2021 ), infilling models (cite96†Yoo et al., 2023a ), neural lexical substitution (cite97†Qiang et al., 2023 ), or encoder-decoder architectures (cite72†Abdelnabi and Fritz, 2021 ; cite98†Zhang et al., 2024 ; cite99†Xu et al., 2024 ).\nL116: ### 2.2 Generation-Time Watermarking With Large Language Models\nL117: The first watermarks for machine-generated text date back to a method presumably used in Google Translate to filter automated translations from future training data (cite100†Venugopal et al., 2011 ). For LLM-generated text, two concurrent approaches appeared shortly after the release of ChatGPT. cite77†Kirchenbauer et al. (2023a) bias a subset of the vocabulary, while cite76†Aaronson and Kirchner (2023) alter the sampling via the Gumbel trick.\nL118: Both use pseudorandom seeds generated from a secret key and preceding tokens, enabling lightweight detection through statistical tests without access to the model.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPixel Seal: Adversarial-only training for invisible image and video watermarking (https://arxiv.org/html/2512.16874v1)\nciteturn6465view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.16874v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 408\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLearning to Watermark in the Latent Space of Generative Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.16140v1)\nciteturn6465view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2601.16140v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 589\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2510.12812] We Can Hide More Bits: The Unused Watermarking Capacity in Theory and in Practice (https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.12812)\nciteturn6465view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.12812\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 161\n"
